# lazyit reverse proxy — Caddy. See ADR-0026, ADR-0037.
#
# Two HTTPS site blocks:
#   1. {$LAZYIT_SITE_ADDRESS} — the lazyit web app + API (same-origin routing, ADR-0026).
#   2. auth.{$LAZYIT_DOMAIN}  — Zitadel IdP; Caddy auto-TLS covers it (ADR-0037).
#
# Automatic HTTPS: Caddy's INTERNAL CA for `localhost` (local prod-like), and Let's Encrypt
# for a real domain. Routing is SAME-ORIGIN: the browser calls /api/* and Caddy forwards it
# to the API, so the web image is domain-portable (NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=/api, baked at build).
{
	admin off
	# Real domain (Let's Encrypt): set an ACME contact email. Leave commented for local.
	# email {$LAZYIT_TLS_EMAIL}

	# Serve this site's certificate to clients that send NO SNI (issue #1010). This is what makes a
	# bare-IP deployment work (LAZYIT_SITE_ADDRESS=192.168.1.50): TLS SNI is a HOSTNAME field, so
	# browsers AND curl/OpenSSL (RFC 6066) never send an IP literal as SNI. Without SNI, Caddy can't
	# match the IP site block to select a cert and aborts the handshake with an internal-error alert
	# (SSL_ERROR_INTERNAL_ERROR_ALERT / "tlsv1 alert internal error"). default_sni names the ServerName
	# to assume when SNI is absent, so the IP cert is presented and connect-by-IP works (the common
	# small-team case, ADR-0086). Additive + harmless for hostname/localhost deploys: those clients DO
	# send SNI, so this is a no-op for them.
	default_sni {$LAZYIT_SITE_ADDRESS:localhost}

	# Trusted proxies (SEC-010). Caddy is the ONLY hop in front of the API, and api/web/db sit on a
	# private Docker network. `static private_ranges` tells Caddy to trust X-Forwarded-For only from
	# private-range peers (the Docker network) and to use the real connecting client IP otherwise —
	# so a public caller's FORGED leftmost X-Forwarded-For is dropped and the value Caddy forwards to
	# the API is trustworthy. The API then sets Express `trust proxy=1` (TRUST_PROXY) so `req.ip` is
	# this verified client, which keys the /config/setup rate-limiter + the first-run audit IP.
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static private_ranges
	}
}

# --- Reusable snippets ------------------------------------------------------
# Baseline response security headers applied to every site. Defensive defaults that do not
# depend on the deployment hostname (so they're safe on localhost AND a real domain):
#   - X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff  — stop MIME-type sniffing.
#   - Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin — don't leak full URLs cross-origin.
#   - X-Frame-Options: DENY — defense-in-depth against clickjacking (CSP frame-ancestors is the
#     modern control; this covers older browsers without weakening anything).
# `-Server` strips Caddy's Server header (don't advertise the proxy/version).
(security_headers) {
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		-Server
	}
}

# HSTS — enable ONLY for a real, publicly-trusted domain. Tells browsers to force HTTPS for the
# next year (incl. subdomains). MUST NOT be set on localhost/internal-CA deployments: it would pin
# the developer's browser to HTTPS-only for `localhost` across every project. Uncomment the
# `import hsts` line in the site block below once you serve on a Let's-Encrypt FQDN.
(hsts) {
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
}

# JSON access log to stdout — captured by Docker's json-file driver + rotation
# (infra/docker-compose.prod.yml). Structured logs pair with the API's Pino logs (ADR-0031).
(access_log) {
	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}
}

# --- Container healthcheck endpoint (internal only) --------------------------------
# A plain-HTTP liveness site on :2021, reachable ONLY from inside the container (this port is
# never published in compose, so it is not a public surface). The compose `healthcheck` probes it
# with BusyBox wget — which the alpine image ships but which CANNOT skip TLS verification
# (no --no-check-certificate) nor stop following redirects, so it can't usefully probe the real
# :443 site (internal CA) or :80 (308 -> https). This dedicated http:// listener sidesteps both:
# it confirms the Caddy process is up and serving config without depending on the api/web upstreams.
http://:2021 {
	respond /healthz "ok" 200
}

# --- Zitadel IdP — auth subdomain (ADR-0037, gated by ADR-0086) ------------------
# The auth.{domain} site block lives in sites/auth.caddy and is imported here ONLY when that file is
# present in the container. infra/docker-compose.oidc.yaml mounts it (bundled Zitadel / AUTH_MODE=oidc);
# in local-auth mode (the default) and BYOI the oidc overlay isn't loaded, sites/ is empty, this glob
# matches nothing (NOT an error — Caddy docs), and Caddy never serves auth.{domain} nor requests an
# unusable cert for it. `import` at top level can bring in a whole site block; it is textual + resolved
# before parse, so sites/auth.caddy can reference the (security_headers)/(access_log) snippets above.
import sites/*.caddy

# Site address — one block, three shapes driven by LAZYIT_SITE_ADDRESS (ADR-0026, ADR-0087):
#   - unset / "localhost"          -> internal-CA HTTPS on :443 (host :8443).            [local]
#   - "lazyit.example.com" (FQDN)  -> publicly reachable :80/:443 (Let's Encrypt).       [real]
#   - ":80" (PORT-ONLY)            -> plain HTTP for ANY Host on that port, NO TLS, no    [lan]
#                                     cert, no http->https redirect. This is what makes a
#                                     host-agnostic LAN deploy work (survives a DHCP IP change).
# The @api/handle routing below is scheme-agnostic, and default_sni/trusted_proxies are no-ops
# without TLS — so this single block serves all three unchanged (verified with `caddy validate`).
{$LAZYIT_SITE_ADDRESS:localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip

	import security_headers
	import access_log

	# HSTS — UNCOMMENT only on a real, publicly-trusted (Let's Encrypt) FQDN. Leave commented for
	# the local prod-like / internal-CA stack: HSTS on `localhost` pins the developer's browser to
	# HTTPS-only for every localhost project. See the (hsts) snippet note above.
	# import hsts

	# Swagger UI / raw OpenAPI (/api/docs*) is NO LONGER forwarded on the public origin (SEC-009).
	# The OpenAPI doc enumerates the full authenticated attack surface and was the one anonymous
	# surface once DEF-001 closed (every route now needs a Bearer JWT). The API also stops serving it
	# in production (NODE_ENV gate, main.ts). Docs remain reachable on the internal Docker network and
	# in local dev. There is intentionally no @apidocs handle here: a public /api/docs* now falls to
	# @api below, which strips /api -> /docs* and the API does not serve that path (Swagger mounts at
	# /api/docs, unstripped) -> 404, never a docs page.

	# /api/* -> API, stripping the /api prefix (e.g. /api/users -> /users), EXCEPT Auth.js
	# routes (/api/auth/*) which the Next.js web app owns (ADR-0039). They are excluded here
	# and fall through to the catch-all below -> web:3000.
	#
	# Why not a dedicated `handle @authjs { reverse_proxy web:3000 }`? It would be byte-identical
	# to the catch-all, and Caddy's Caddyfile adapter MERGES identical handle blocks — silently
	# dropping the /api/auth/* matcher, so those requests fall to @api and hit the API as 404.
	# Verified with `caddy adapt`. Excluding the path from @api is the reliable construction.
	@api {
		path /api/*
		not path /api/auth/*
	}
	handle @api {
		uri strip_prefix /api
		reverse_proxy api:3001
	}

	# Everything else — the web UI AND Auth.js's /api/auth/* — -> the Next.js web app.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy web:3000
	}
}
